[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[vset VERSION 0.1]
[manpage_begin  map::area::file n [vset VERSION]]
[category {Map Display and Supporting Utilities}][keywords map]
[keywords {file, geo/area}]
[keywords {geo/area, file}]
[moddesc   {Map display support}]
[titledesc {Reading/writing tklib geo/area files}]
[require Tcl 8.6-]
[require debug]
[require debug::caller]
[require map::slippy 0.8]
[require snit]
[require  map::area::file [opt [vset VERSION]]]
[description]

This package provides commands to read and write Tklib [term geo/area] files. The format of these
files is described in section [sectref {File format}], the value returned by [cmd {map area file read}]
and accepted by [cmd {map area file write}] in section [sectref {geo/area value}].

[para] Packages using these files are [package map::area::store::fs], which loads all
[term geo/area] files of a directory, and through it [package map::area::store::memory].

[section API]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd {::map area file read}] [arg path]]

Reads the [term geo/area] file [arg path] and returns its contents as a [term geo/area] value, see
section [sectref {geo/area value}].

[para] If the file cannot be opened, or is not a valid [term geo/area] file, the result is the
empty string. No error is thrown in these cases.

[para] If the file does not specify any name, the area gets a single name, the name of the file
without directory and extension. For example, an unnamed area in file [file /data/park.area] is
named [const park].

[call [cmd {::map area file write}] [arg path] [arg area]]

Writes the [term geo/area] value [arg area] (see section [sectref {geo/area value}]) to the file
[arg path], replacing any previous contents. The names are written in dictionary order, with
duplicates removed. The coordinates are written in the order given, and are not validated.

[para] The result of the command is the empty string. Failure to open the file is reported as an
error.

[list_end]

[section {geo/area value}]

A [term geo/area] is a polygon on the surface of Earth, the outline of some area, together with a
list of names. It is represented by a Tcl dictionary with the following keys:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const names]]
A list of strings, the names of the area. Several names can be specified, e.g. aliases or names in
different languages.

[def [const geo]]
A list of geographical locations, the vertices of the polygon, in order. Each location is a list of
two numbers, the latitude and the longitude in degrees, in this order (the [term geo] of
[package map::slippy]). The polygon is implicitly closed, the last vertex is connected to the first.
[list_end]

For example:

[example {names {{Lost Lagoon}} geo {{49.2963 -123.1461} {49.2981 -123.1432} {49.2955 -123.1391}}}]

[section {File format}]

A [term geo/area] file is a text file, read line by line:

[list_begin itemized]
[item] Leading and trailing whitespace of each line is ignored.

[item] Empty lines are ignored.

[item] Lines starting with [const #] or [const //] are comments, and ignored.

[item] The first line which is neither empty nor a comment must be the magic line
[const tklib/geo/area], identifying the file.

[item] Every other line which is not a number is a name of the area. There may be any number of
names, including none. A name cannot look like a floating point number, it would be read as a
coordinate.

[item] Every line which is a number is a coordinate. The coordinates are taken in pairs, the latitude
followed by the longitude of a vertex of the polygon. The latitude must be in the range -90 to 90,
the longitude in the range -180 to 180 degrees. At least one vertex (two coordinates) is required,
and the number of coordinates must be even.

[list_end]

[para] By convention the names are written after the magic line and before the coordinates, and
files use the extension [file .area]. This is also the default file pattern of
[package map::area::store::fs].

[para] For example:

[example {tklib/geo/area
# Lost Lagoon, Stanley Park, Vancouver
Lost Lagoon
49.2963
-123.1461
49.2981
-123.1432
49.2955
-123.1391}]

[section EXAMPLE]

[example_begin]
package require map::area::file

map area file write lagoon.area {
    names {{Lost Lagoon}}
    geo   {{49.2963 -123.1461} {49.2981 -123.1432} {49.2955 -123.1391}}
}

set area [lb]map area file read lagoon.area[rb]
puts [lb]dict get $area names[rb]       ;# -> {Lost Lagoon}
puts [lb]llength [lb]dict get $area geo[rb][rb]  ;# -> 3
[example_end]

